# ─── ONE HOME FOR THE SITE (must be first) ──────────────────
# The Netlify subdomain serves a full, indexable copy of the site. Without
# this, Google can index it as a rival website and links to it leak away.
https://lighthearted-sunburst-4f0ba2.netlify.app/*  https://foreverprint.com/:splat  301!

# Netlify URL rewriting (Phase 5 — landing pages + configurator)
#
# Format: /<from>  /<to>  <status>
#   Status 200 = rewrite (URL stays clean in browser)
#   Status 301 = permanent redirect (browser navigates to new URL)
#
# IMPORTANT: Netlify only applies these rules when no real file exists at the
# requested path. So /header.html, /footer.html etc. are still served from disk.
# Rules are processed top-to-bottom; the first match wins.

# ─── ORDER PATHS (must come BEFORE the slug catch-all) ──────
# /<slug>/order → configurator. The :slug placeholder captures the slug part
# and passes it to upload-and-print.html as a query param.
# E.g. /wedding-invitations/order → /upload-and-print.html?product=wedding-invitations

/:slug/order  /upload-and-print.html?product=:slug  200

# ─── KNOWN SITE PAGES (give them clean URLs) ────────────────
# Each line: clean URL → actual file. These come before the product catch-all.

/products       /products.html         200
/admin          /admin.html            200
/design-studio  /design-studio.html    200
/how-it-works   /how-it-works.html     200
/track-order    /track-order.html      200

# ─── PRODUCT LANDING CATCH-ALL ──────────────────────────────
# Any URL not matched above and with no real file → product landing page
# (NEW behaviour — was the configurator before). The landing page reads the
# slug from the URL and looks it up in product_types Supabase table:
#   • Match found → renders that product's landing page (hero, pricing, etc.)
#   • No match → renders 404 state with link back to /products
#
# Customers click "Configure & Order" on the landing page → /:slug/order →
# configurator (handled by the rule at the top of this file).
#
# This rule means you NEVER need to edit _redirects when adding a new product.
# Adding "Order of Service" in admin → publishing → /order-of-service just works
# AND so does /order-of-service/order automatically.

# ─── Real files serve directly (never rewritten to product.html) ───
# Without these, the /* catch-all below rewrites image/asset requests to
# product.html, so images come back as HTML and render blank.
/*.jpg   /:splat.jpg   200
/*.jpeg  /:splat.jpeg  200
/*.png   /:splat.png   200
/*.svg   /:splat.svg   200
/*.webp  /:splat.webp  200
/*.gif   /:splat.gif   200
/*.css   /:splat.css   200
/*.js    /:splat.js    200
/*.ico   /:splat.ico   200
/*.woff  /:splat.woff  200
/*.woff2 /:splat.woff2 200
/*.pdf   /:splat.pdf   200

# Internal documents are NOT public. The publish directory is the repository
# root, so every committed file is served — docs/PRICING.md would hand anyone
# our cost base, the supplier discount and our margin position at a plain URL.
# The trailing ! forces the rule even though the file exists, which is the only
# way to stop Netlify serving it. Verify after deploying: /docs/PRICING.md and
# /BRAND-IMAGERY-GUIDE.md must both return 404.
/docs/*   /404.html   404!
/*.md     /404.html   404!

# Anything that reaches here does not exist. It used to fall through to
# product.html, which answered HTTP 200 with "Product not found" — a soft 404.
# Google treats that as a real page: it indexes junk URLs, wastes crawl budget
# and never learns the address is dead. Verified before the change: /guides,
# /guides/test and /this-page-does-not-exist all returned 200.
#
# Product pages are real generated files (wedding-invitations.html and the
# rest), which Netlify serves for the clean path without needing a rule, so
# nothing depends on the old catch-all.
/*  /404.html  404
